= Related Work

== Large Language Models for Code

Models trained on source code, such as Codex [11], Code Llama [9], StarCoder [10] and DeepSeek-Coder [12], are good at writing and editing code in a single file or function. They can only read a limited amount of text at once, and they use information in the middle of a long input less reliably [13]. A whole repository is far larger than that limit, so a retrieval step must choose what the model sees.

== Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for Repositories

Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) [3] means fetching relevant text and giving it to a language model. For code, RepoCoder [4] retrieves and generates iteratively to complete code inside a repository, and the RepoBench benchmark [5] measures cross-file retrieval. BM25 [1] is a classic keyword ranking method and is still a strong baseline. Dense passage retrieval [14] uses learned vectors instead of keywords, and rank fusion [2] is a standard way to merge ranked lists from different methods. Latent semantic analysis (LSA) [6] builds word-meaning vectors from word co-occurrence without a neural network, which is the approach we use for our "vector" ranker.

== AST-Based and Graph-Based Code Analysis

An abstract syntax tree (AST) is a tree that represents the structure of a source file. Parsers such as Tree-sitter [8] build ASTs from which symbols (functions, classes, variables) and relationships (imports, calls) can be read directly. Graph representations of programs, such as program dependence graphs [17] and code property graphs [16], are well established in compilers and vulnerability detection. CodePlan [15] uses dependency graphs to plan edits across a repository. This is not a complete survey; a fuller comparison with other graph-based code retrieval systems is left to a later version.

= Motivation and Problem Formulation

== Motivating Scenario

Consider the question "Trace what happens when a user connects a GitHub repository by URL" asked about this project's backend. The behaviour passes through four files: the route (`routes/repo.routes.js`), the controller (`controllers/repo.controller.js`), the service (`services/repo.service.js`) and a Git helper (`services/git.service.js`). Three dependency edges connect them.

A plain keyword search finds files that contain the words "repository" or "GitHub". Those words also appear in unrelated database code, and the route-to-controller edge shares almost no words with the question. A graph traversal that follows every dependency from the best-matching files returns many edges and buries the three that matter. Table 1 shows what the methods in this study actually returned for this question.

