*Flow methods.* Each returns a set of file-to-file edges (see Table 6 for the full list): unpruned 2-hop traversal; the top 3 edges by path words; the top 3 edges by BM25; Graph Path Pruning with path relevance, with BM25 relevance (our *primary* variant, chosen before the held-out run) and with fused-ranking relevance (chosen afterwards, so exploratory); and the primary variant with the layer bonus or the continuity bonus switched off.

*Not included.* We did not build a language-model-only baseline, and we did not build a "function relationships" question category (callers and callees).

== Evaluation Metrics

#tbl([What the scores mean. Higher is better for every score. In each results column the best value is bold on a green background.])[
  #plain-table(
    (1.4fr, 4fr),
    ([*Score*], [*Plain meaning*]),
    [Ranked 1st], [Share of questions where a correct file is the very first result.],
    [MRR (mean reciprocal rank)], [Average of 1 divided by the position of the first correct file. 1.00 means always first; 0.50 means about second; 0.10 means about tenth.],
    [Found in top 10], [Of all the files that were correct, the share that appear among the first ten results.],
    [nDCG\@10], [Like "found in top 10" but also rewards putting correct files nearer the top. 1.00 is a perfect ordering.],
    [Precision, recall, F1], [Used for flows. Precision: of the edges we return, the share that are correct. Recall: of the correct edges, the share we return. F1 combines the two into one balanced score (0 to 1).],
    [95% interval], [A range around an average. If we repeated the experiment on similar questions, the true average would fall inside it about 95 times in 100. A wide range means few questions or noisy results.],
  )
]

The exact formulas are in Appendix A.1. Locate and dependency questions use Hit\@k and MRR over the top 7 results; impact questions use Recall\@10 and nDCG\@10; flows use precision, recall and F1 over edges.

*Statistics.* Intervals are 95% bootstrap intervals (we resample the questions 10,000 times for flows and 2,000 times for retrieval tables). Differences between methods are tested with a paired sign-flip permutation test, which is exact when there are 18 or fewer non-zero differences and uses a fixed-seed random approximation otherwise. A p-value below 0.0001 is written as "p < 0.0001". We run many tests and do not correct for that.

